{
  "image_name": "2f6e53322665d8897223f806c6b34159.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three sequencing-based ways to measure translation by tracking ribosomes on mRNA. In all cases, mRNA is drawn as a blue strand and ribosomes as gray particles, but the methods differ in what ribosome-associated RNA is isolated and what information is recovered.\n\nOne approach, TRAP-seq, uses an epitope-tagged ribosome and antibody pull-down to immunoprecipitate ribosome-bound mRNAs, then sequence them. This identifies transcripts associated with tagged ribosomes but does not show ribosome positions at codon-level detail.\n\nA second approach, steady-state ribosome profiling, chemically freezes translating ribosomes with cycloheximide, digests unprotected RNA, and sequences the remaining ribosome-protected fragments. Aligning these short fragments to a reference transcript or genome produces peaks across coding regions, revealing where ribosomes sit during ongoing translation between start and stop codons.\n\nThe third approach is dynamic ribosome profiling. It first traps ribosomes at initiation codons using harringtonine, then after time intervals allows elongating ribosomes to move before freezing them with cycloheximide and digesting RNA. Sequencing over successive time points shifts the ribosome footprint peaks along the transcript, providing a time-resolved view of translation initiation and ribosome translocation.",
